  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>
	<p>Just like massive objects store gravitational potential energy, charged objects store <term>electric potential energy.</term></p>
	<definition xml:id="def-electric_potential_energy">
		<title>Electric Potential Energy</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>The change in <term>electric potential energy</term> when a test charge <m>q_{\text{test}}</m> moves through an electric potential difference <m>\Delta V</m> is written: <me>\Delta U_E = q_{\mathrm{test}}\Delta V</me>
				</p>
		</statement>
	</definition>

	<p>This definition of potential energy intentionally mirrors the definition of electric force <m>\vec{F}^E = q_{\mathrm{test}}\vec{E}</m>.  The new quantity <m>\Delta V</m> is known as the electric potential difference and is based on a new scalar field <m>V</m>, known as the electric potential, which, like the electric field, is created by the presence of other electric charges.  <!--A charged object will experience a potential difference as it moves through, or changes locations in, an electric potential scalar field.--></p>

	<definition xml:id="def_epd">
	<title>
	Electric Potential Difference
	</title>
		<statement>
		
			<p>
			
			</p>
			
			<p>
			The <term> electric potential (difference)</term> is a <em> scalar field </em> that influences the energy of a system with charged objects.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The electric potential is a scalar.</alert>  It can be positive or negative, and is measured in volts (V).
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The electric potential difference is sometimes called voltage.</alert>  Any voltage is referring to an electric potential difference.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The electric potential has a value at every point in space.</alert>  The value can be different at different points in space.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The electric potential is measured relative to somewhere.</alert>  The electric potential, like energy, is only meaningfully expressed as a difference between two points.  It is typical to choose a reference location where the potential is zero and then measure the electric potential relative to that point.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</statement>
</definition>
	
	

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		<activity>
      <title>Sensemaking: Units</title>
      <p></p>
			<p>Express the units of the electric potential (volts) in terms of other units with which you are familiar.</p>
		</activity>
		<activity>
			<title>Sensemaking: Numbers</title>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				An alternative unit for energy is the electron-volt (eV): the amount of potential energy change experienced by one electron moving through an electric potential difference of <m>1 \mathrm{~V}</m>.  Use the definition of the <xref ref="def-electric_potential_energy"/> to determine the number of Joules in an electron-volt.
			</p>
		</activity>
		</subsubsection>
	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

